TECHNICAL WORKING GROUP REPORT

SUBJECT:  NATIONAL SCHOOL FEEDING PROGRAM ACT
	       Substitute Bill of House Bills No. 364, 624, 680, 
                    1154, 1347, 1452, 1504, 1548 and 2627

On 23 April 2014, the technical working group (TWG), chaired by Rep. Pryde Henry A. Teves, convened and deliberated on the substitute bill on National School Feeding Program (NSFP) Act.  Participating in the TWG meeting were resource persons representing the Department of Education (DepEd), Department of Trade and Industry (DTI), National Economic and Development Authority (NEDA), National Dairy Authority (NDA), National Food Authority (NFA) and the National Nutrition Council (NNC).    

The TWG agreed on, among others, the following salient features of the substitute bill:

(1)	  Adoption of the short title, â€œNational School Feeding Program Actâ€�;

(2)	Coverage of NSFP to include public kindergarten and elementary pupils only;

(3)	DepEd as the lead agency in the implementation of the NSFP and to be supported by the participating national government agencies as provided for in their specific tasks enumerated in the substitute bill;

(4)	Mechanics of the NSFP, specifically on

(a)	the period of  one hundred twenty (120) school days as the prescribed duration of the annual school feeding program; 
(b)	required training to all DepEd personnel involved;
(c)	conduct of simultaneous health examinations, including de-worming and vaccination activities to participating  public school children;
(d)	provision of (i) fresh milk-based and fortified foods, and (ii) vitamin supplements to all school children beneficiaries; and
(e)	ensure that the beneficiary school children shall eat their meals and drink their milk inside the classroom only;

(5)	 Utilization of locally-produced fresh milk and locally-available food supplies as priority in the procurement of food supplies including the tapping of the services of local farmers associations and cooperatives for the procurement of locally-produced food supplies;

(6)	Prioritization of municipalities and cities which are determined by competent authorities to have high malnutrition rates in the implementation of the NSFP in case of shortage of funds;

(7)	Encourage the participation of LGU officials and Parent-Teacher Associations;

(8)	Tax exemptions to donations purposely for the NSFP; and

(9)	Regular funding support through the annual General Appropriations Act.

The DSWD, NEDA and NDA have officially submitted their respective position papers, the inputs of which are likewise incorporated in the substitute bill. 

